course: dictionary
lesson: 10297
title: Context clues in sport
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A touchline is one of the two long side lines of a football, rugby or hockey pitch. «The ball went over the touchline.» Even if you have never met the word, the ball going over a line tells you it is a line at the edge of the pitch.
explain: Sport words can have more than one meaning, and the words around them show which one is meant. «On the touchline» means standing at the side of the pitch, where coaches and parents watch. The word pitch is the clue here: the phrase is about a place, not a line you can draw.
explain: Try the check: swap your meaning into the sentence. «When the ball goes over the touchline, play stops.» Does the meaning fit? Yes, because the ball has crossed the line and is out of play.
fact: A touchline is one of the two long side lines of a pitch.
fact: «Touchline» is used in football, rugby and hockey.
fact: When the ball goes over the touchline, it is out of play.
fact: «On the touchline» means at the side of the pitch, where coaches and fans stand.
fact: A pitch has two touchlines and two goal lines, so its outside edge is four lines long.
exercise: «The ball rolled over the touchline and out of play.» What is a touchline? | The middle of the pitch | A referee's whistle | A goal, where you score | *One of the long side lines of the pitch
exercise: Mum shouts from the «touchline» during the match. Where is she standing? | In the middle of the pitch | In the changing room | Inside the goal | *At the side of the pitch
exercise: How many touchlines does a pitch have? | None | Four | *Two | One
test: «A fair throw-in uses both hands, from behind and over the head, with part of both feet on or behind the touchline.» Where must the thrower's feet be? | *On or behind the touchline | Up in the stands | In the middle of the pitch | Inside the goal
test: Which phrase means standing at the side of the pitch? | Under the goal | *On the touchline | In the centre circle | Behind the net
exam: Which sentence uses touchline correctly? | Mum read a touchline book last night. | We ate a touchline sandwich at lunch. | *The ball rolled over the touchline and out of play. | The touchline sold us tickets at the gate.
